          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My father had a heart attack at 55. What does that mean for my own risk, and how does guidance use family history when deciding about a statin?",
          "answer": "**Seek emergency care now.** What you have described can be a medical emergency. Call emergency services or go to an emergency department immediately. Do not wait to see whether it improves, and do not drive yourself.\n\nYour question is about your father's history, not necessarily about symptoms you are having now. If you have chest pain, pressure, or tightness, shortness of breath, or pain spreading to your arm, jaw, or back, get emergency help right away.\n\nOn your general question, the retrieved guidance is limited:\n\n- **Family history and risk:** The lipid screening and statin guidance (major cardiology society consensus guidance) lists family history of premature cardiovascular disease as a \"risk-enhancing feature.\" It does not replace the main risk estimate. It can shift a decision for adults whose estimated 10-year risk falls in the borderline or intermediate range. The coronary artery disease reference also lists a family history of premature coronary disease as a contributor.\n- **How the statin decision is made:** That guidance estimates 10-year cardiovascular risk from age, sex, cholesterol, blood pressure, blood pressure treatment, diabetes, and smoking status. For adults aged 40 to 75 without established disease, risk is grouped as low (below 5%), borderline (5% to below 7.5%), intermediate (7.5% to below 20%), or high (20% or above). Where the decision is still uncertain, the guidance mentions a coronary artery calcium score.\n- **What the sources do not cover:** The passages did not define what counts as \"premature,\" did not say how much a father's heart attack at 55 changes your estimate, and did not give any statin doses or product choices. I can't answer those from these sources.\n\nBecause your father's heart attack is significant family history, bring it up with a clinician. They can work out your own risk using your age, measurements, and history, and they can explain whether a statin discussion makes sense for you.\n\n*Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.*",
